right hi everyone so I'm Jacky Guttman  I'm gonna talk today a little bit about  an approach to data science programming  that we use at Flatiron health to help  us continuously monitor data quality in  our oncology healthcare data and so  specifically data quality of dynamic  longitudinal data and so I'll kind of  quickly introduce myself and the  challenge that we've been addressing in  kind of maintaining very high standards  for data quality in data sets that are  continually being updated refreshed and  redeliver each month and then I'll show  how some approaches to structuring the  data and taking a kind of functional  approach to code development helps us to  mitigate and simplify that challenge  there's plenty of seats like all over so  make yourself comfortable okay so first  a little bit about me I joined Flatiron  health in January after doing data  science in machine learning and a few  different industries including medical  education public school education meal  kit delivery services so now I do the  same thing only but for oncology and  clinical care so just a little bit about  the sort of problems and type of data  that I'm dealing with here  Flatiron Health is a company that  strives to improve lives by learning  from the experience of every cancer  patient so practically speaking that  means a lot of data right including data  on over 2 million cancer patients in the  United States and that data gets updated  on a continual basis and every month we  freeze and deliver that data to partners  and clients and we deliver it with a 30  day recency so meaning that the time  from a patient experience or outcome  being observed in the  chanak health record to the time that  that observation is in the hands of  researchers and industry leaders who can  generate insights from it and is only  one month which is really considered  unparalleled in the world of medical  research and real world evidence so  something that will become very relevant  to the data quality approach that we're  taking here is that our company relies  on close collaboration between technical  staff with expertise in building  managing and testing data pipelines  statistical and methodological experts  who really love to dig into and evaluate  the data and and clinical experts in  oncology who help us to translate what  we're seeing in the data to what that  means in the real world for patients  with cancer and oncology providers that  are being described by the data and so a  little bit about where the data is  coming to us from our data points come  to us from a wide array of sources  including the more than 800 unique sites  of care that use our electronic health  record technology as well as several  academic medical centers which each use  their own electronic health record  software and and they partner with us on  converting that data into outcomes  research so our one of our major data  products are these kind of national  longitudinal data sets of real-world  oncology data which we update and grow  each month so we currently deliver  datasets on 20 distinct cancer types  these datasets have some shared but non  identical data structures and part of  monitoring the quality of our data over  time includes tracking the changes to  our data delivery from this month as  compared to the previous month as  compared to through months prior or six  months prior and really being able to  understand and draw conclusions about  the quality and consistency of the data  for each type of cancer as compared to  other data marts that we've built out  and continue to maintain and  so what does this mean for us what is  the challenge we need a framework for  detecting and monitoring unusual changes  within a cross variables within an  across cancer types within and across  patients when we're looking at and  comparing kind of different snapshots of  our data frozen at distinct points in  time so we're not just looking at  analyzing a single data set but we're  looking at analyzing many different data  sets in tandem and sort of understanding  the quality of each one relative to the  related data sets until we need to be  able to stand up any kind of data  quality framework on an ongoing basis  right so needs to being able to easily  be refreshed and updated throughout the  month so that we can make changes to our  data pipeline as needed and we're doing  this as the number of data elements  within each data set are growing as well  as the number of unique data sets  overall are growing and growing at a  pretty rapid pace so in addition to that  kind of challenge we also really need to  have reliable and readable code that can  be collaboratively developed by  technical and non-technical and clinical  staff all working together to kind of  synthesize the methodological best  practices with the clinical intuition of  our research oncology experts so there's  many different frameworks and tools that  go into this for us but I want to  specifically focus on some of the more  generalizable learnings here that a few  perspective shifts that kind of make  this comparative evaluation of our data  possible and make it possible in a sort  of maintainable and scalable way so in  order to meet this challenge we knew we  would benefit from really codifying  that statistical and domain knowledge  into a library of modular functions  and we felt that these functions should  be testable they should clear an intent  and they should be able to be composed  into higher-level more abstract  processes and building out these  function libraries sort of serves to  both document and kind of enforce what  we on the quantitative sciences team  established as best practices for  working with our data um additionally  for the purposes of maintainability and  fostering collaboration we wanted to be  able to decouple the high level sort of  checks and reasoning represented by the  code from the low level implementation  details to make those checks really more  transparent and interpretable to anybody  looking at the analysis code and finally  we wanted to ensure a flexible approach  that could take reasoning in code  originally developed for working with a  single data set and kind of seamlessly  extend it to analyzing many data sets  simultaneously and without breaking down  when the structure of a new data set  differs a minor but meaningful ways from  historical data or from data derived  from a different cancer type or patient  population so the way we approach this  problem really borrows a lot from the  principles of functional programming and  I think it's pretty easy for discussions  of functional programming to quickly  devolve into computer science jargon but  for our purposes in thinking about data  science and data quality evaluation in  particular the definition really means a  few key things so most importantly  functions lots of functions and kind of  functions as first order citizens that  we can treat has just like any other  variable and pass these functions it has  inputs to other functions so that allows  us to kind of string together these  functions into higher-order functions  that return new functions depending on  the conditions and context of how we're  using them and next that these functions  or chains of functions with very clear  declarative name  kind of give us an additional degree of  separation from the mechanics of the  implementation and to allow us to sort  of abstract out what is being done from  precisely how that process is being  carried out under the hood and then  finally that these functions should  strive for some degree of purity in that  they avoid side effects and dependencies  on variables and states outside of the  world of the function so this gives us a  kind of sense of confidence that no  matter what else is in our environment  no matter what order we run the function  or how many times we call it if we  provide the same input into the function  we're go