 so not just  whether it's implemented correctly so  this style of programming can support a  team structure where those with a  significant problem and domain expertise  can kind of undertake a sort of  conceptual code review and those with  considerable technical expertise can  really focus on an implementation code  review so here's a little example of how  these nested data structures I spoke  about might work in practice so we can  build out a data frame that refers to  several firsts and snapshots of data  taken at different points in time from  different database schemas and tables  and possibly of different disease types  in this case and because data frames are  the sort of fundamental structure that  we're used to working with this kind of  comes naturally to us and we can now  treat individual data sets as we would  individual observations within a data  frame and kind of hold databases of many  data sets with their associated metadata  as just any typical data set that we  want to you understand and analyze so  this kind of allows us to take a kind of  simple function that we have developed  as a data scientist to take in as input  a single data set and output a single  evaluation metric  and create from that a sort of set of  higher-order functions that return sort  of composite evaluation metrics and look  at kind of changes in the quality and  those evaluation metrics over time or  between data sets and we can do this  kind of rather seamlessly across many  dimensions and many data sets because  this is all stored within this kind of  parent data frame so for example here  for we might take a very sort of broad  and generic function that executes a  query against our database and we might  use the kind of notion of partial  functions to sort of fill in some of the  arguments to that function to make it do  something a little bit more specific  right so rather than just execute any  query we create a more specific version  of that that execute the query against  the database that counts all unique  patients in a data set or execute a  query against the database to return a  list of all of the patients in that data  set meaning a specific inclusion  criteria and we can apply that function  to all the nested data sets within our  parent data frame the same way we might  apply a function to all rows of a  particular data frame and and again you  know each data set might have tens or  hundreds of thousands of observations  but the total number of data sets is  probably in the dozens at most right and  so we're not really particularly worried  about kind of vectorization here right  we're talking about something  higher-level that takes in as input an  entire data set rather than a single  observation or set of observations so  starting from this structure we can sort  of begin to add complexity by chaining  some of the functions together into  larger data pipelines or we can build  you in a kind of error handling to these  component functions but the functions  themselves really remain pretty easy to  develop for  data scientists because each function is  still at its core usually taking in  either a single data set or perhaps a  pair of data sets to be compared so I'll  go through some more kind of concrete  examples of what this approach to  programming for data quality gets us but  we found the benefits can really be kind  of broken down along these five  dimensions so first and foremost  readability right this approach really  lends itself to readable code higher  level abstractions that emphasize  declarative intent over procedural  instructions are more easily understood  and sanity checked by our non-technical  staff so that supports the kind of  differentiated code review we spoke  about earlier it's really clear to kind  of look at this and see what it's doing  even if we're not sure about the actual  details of the implementation so this  also provides a kind of self documenting  code where the function and