ucibility so we  want to really have confidence that for  any analysis running the same function  with the same input should always  produce the same result so by developing  functions where we avoid mutability  we're calling the function changes the  actual state or value variables defined  outside of the function and preventing  other similar kinds of side effects we  can really avoid hard to test for bugs  and we don't want so this the success or  failure of our unit test to depend on  things outside of the actual function  and its input so so this this concept of  immutable inputs and outputs and  processes that you know are  deterministic and always return the same  result when given the same inputs that's  going to help increase the sort of  reproducibility of our code and then  closely related to this as I kind of  alluded to is this idea of robustness so  modular functional code that avoids this  kind of mutability and side-effects and  external dependencies it's going to be  easier to test and if we're using unit  testing more effectively we're gonna be  building more robust code in the long  run so code that is harder to test just  won't get tested or won't get tested  effectively and so it will be more prone  to unexpected errors but that's  unexpected errors what about expected  errors the functional approach can  actually help us here too by wrapping  sort of unsafe brittle functions with no  exception handling wrapping them in  their own sort of try catch exception  handlers where the new exception handled  version of the function gets returned as  a  you newly-named more robust function and  that robust function can then be sort of  mindlessly deployed in an analysis  without breaking the functional flow of  the code or requiring the analyst to  think deeply about potential exceptions  that might occur each time they're using  the function so again the tools library  here provides us with the excepts  wrapper to turn any coloboma into sort  of a more 